DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ffggeettss() function reads at most one less than the number of characters
     specified by _s_i_z_e from the given _s_t_r_e_a_m and stores them in the string
     _s_t_r.  Reading stops when a newline character is found, at end-of-file or
     error.  The newline, if any, is retained.  If any characters are read and
     there is no error, a ‘\0’ character is appended to end the string.

     The ggeettss__ss() function is equivalent to ffggeettss() with a _s_t_r_e_a_m of stdin,
     except that the newline character (if any) is not stored in the string.

     The ggeettss() function was unsafe and is no longer available.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     Upon successful completion, ffggeettss() and ggeettss__ss() return a pointer to the
     string.  If end-of-file occurs before any characters are read, they re‐
     turn NULL and the buffer contents remain unchanged.  If an error occurs,
     they return NULL and the buffer contents are indeterminate.  The ffggeettss()
     and ggeettss__ss() functions do not distinguish between end-of-file and error,
     and callers must use feof(3) and ferror(3) to determine which occurred.

